# SYNOPSIS

```usage
whoami
```

# DESCRIPTION

`whoami` prints the name of the user the process runs as, looked up by its effective user id in the C library's users database, `/etc/passwd`.

`whoami` is sbase's, built into `sbase-box` with sbase's other commands and installed as `/boot/bin/posix/whoami`, a hard link to it.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so POSIX programs find it as `/bin/whoami`.
There is no native `whoami`.

# SEE ALSO

logname(1), users(6); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/whoami.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

A user id with no entry is reported as `whoami: getpwuid` <uid>`: no such user`, or with the error that stopped the lookup, and `whoami` exits 1.
Any argument prints the usage message and exits 1.

# BUGS

`whoami` cannot work on VectraOS: the C library gives every process the user id 0, and the POSIX namespace has no `/etc/passwd`; VectraOS's users are in its own database, users(6), which the C library does not read.
It is expected to fail as above; what it says is untested.
